#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124191 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "da9684b4-df8d-531d-a200-cf54685067c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10913" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf01827dc23a26ed9d8d81333d735021d3a4eb494a434ba9fc38f1d110dfcc25.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x : |\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0| <        \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0| <    \\delta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n![](images/0a/0af9e8b587bbeb640ff9ec0cdc8197a9d99e8f5282772561b92ba74915cf1226.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point because there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124192 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "7e78e3a1-836a-53af-95a3-0a9d192cc7d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10924" :question/problem "![](images/38/38057eabc400c888e287be8dbbd8b4de172b625fa5922188f99f71112213ef3b.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-\\infty, 2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is the set of points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* $\\delta{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\{ x : |  x- x_0| <           \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of $x_0$ is given by the open interval $( x_0-\\delta,  x_0+\\delta),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e3aa5b8e0e93f7fda306a6f39c999af9a587614795316ce12fff8da2c9c2dd2.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/38/389aa307a3c0c10b5d51b86bed6e05331fb9299379652f06e65a4a7b9f776ead.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists any neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained *outside* the set $M.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c0992788a0ac7ce8cab2caabb41479ba2267e532412d1ef2c7115f56afb911c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124199 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "1f21955b-70dc-5b61-8b19-42860de1be4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-11031" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y > 4-2x\\big\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y = 4-2x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(1, 2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(1, 2)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x : |\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0| <                   \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0| <               \\delta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=4-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/8e/8e8e3296c59277b92eddddd3deacd7149fd9d7062aa05f4858e1846c3fb3238b.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/07/077daf974625dd03a02b3f1c55e7b60c5a8c9686bc5515d78246bf7e1048e4cf.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1, 2)$ satisfy the equation $y = 4-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $\\partial A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1, 2)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y > 4-2x.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124200 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "9b01032c-d4e6-5660-a3c9-477e67c011a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11032" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:  \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} \\geq 1 \\bigg\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:  \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} =1 \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(1, 1)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x : |\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0| <                  \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0| <              \\delta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or outside the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} = 1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/d7/d70dec41d9d5d24d82d1efafd65f1898ffcd5794dca98ca46d486cd3be428a18.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1, 1)$ and $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/3e/3e99fed83e9e31f223bfb4c1a9df87aa0b54e7865c1bfbc5b175b684c7c3c58a.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ satisfy the equation $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} = 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $\\partial A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1, 1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} \\geq 1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ Therefore, the point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa6a3646-f0f3-518d-83e8-d652aac9631a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453186} {:answer/id #uuid "abc7b05c-d177-5bd3-b0c0-c7d473c392d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abc7b05c-d177-5bd3-b0c0-c7d473c392d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453187} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ad4d9fe-863c-51fe-b819-b9cd839172fa" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454343} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e3ec6a-8bf8-591c-8390-ac4ee902b844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453188} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c76f95-7d8e-507d-bba0-2592577506ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10e3ec6a-8bf8-591c-8390-ac4ee902b844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453188} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c0619b-af68-5f6a-909b-32abdbd64822" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db96c2a1-daaa-5554-9596-3e9933aa3a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453190} {:answer/id #uuid "bddaa16e-05fb-57e6-8b2a-64267d599ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db96c2a1-daaa-5554-9596-3e9933aa3a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453190} :answer-field/id #uuid "ded76903-a98d-53d0-9797-9231cdcdfee6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab42b0ba-ecca-5e90-984d-305864760335" :question/math-academy-id "q-105336" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}\\ge 36},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(3,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(3,2)$ is a boundary point of $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(3,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or outside the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/67/67d1f23f424561c69973fe5f08ad3ee3a03346f555db8f00fb71406c02a9cdf9.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,2)$ and $Q(3,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/0b/0b3e72b3a65241c4e03716a9c0b534d687c305a92ceef0b92d5c6df8c1b82953.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(3,2)$ satisfy the inequality $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}\\ge 36.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(3,2)$ do not satisfy the equation $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0992868-cb81-5a5a-a4c6-0d14a14cb81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447748} {:answer/id #uuid "64303aea-6ef1-5ea6-b850-8ab554c11d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0992868-cb81-5a5a-a4c6-0d14a14cb81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447748} :answer-field/id #uuid "4daa31ae-12ed-5739-bd66-244779ad38e5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448914} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0763eedf-6145-5f9d-87aa-d9493d59aa17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447750} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f86495-67e2-5f93-aa9d-87902a0502dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8f86495-67e2-5f93-aa9d-87902a0502dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447751} :answer-field/id #uuid "44c1a6ee-5d02-5245-a744-1ad98c34fb95" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448915} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae6eb8a9-adc0-568d-b9f8-4de17f84f813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447752} {:answer/id #uuid "6f897d74-3ced-58fa-9b7e-96bf41790793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae6eb8a9-adc0-568d-b9f8-4de17f84f813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447752} :answer-field/id #uuid "35069c72-c15c-592a-b7bf-a683c1f62c0d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f57db6a6-9edb-5f0f-98dd-62f31492deff" :question/math-academy-id "q-105365" :question/problem "The set $B$ and its boundary $∂B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<12},\\,∂B={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(3,4)$ lies on the boundary of $B$\n- **II.** The point $P(3,4)$ belongs to $B$\n- **III.** The point $Q(-3,6)$ is an interior point of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $B$ consists of all points that lie inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/ad/adee860d39b4d9c4511db2b2f1ad6e7f46342cc474da47303af742be1cc911b3.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,4)$ and $Q(-3,6)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/21/2185e7129cd799eb1cbb888789566a406b1594dd5a649b9129bec491a1dce1c7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(3,4)$ does not satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of $B.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(3,4)$ satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<12.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $B.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $B$ and points that do not belong to $B.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3a1f96d-9a3f-5c1c-ab2d-e8d5123ffa2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459569} {:answer/id #uuid "f84f80cc-e9aa-54c7-90dd-385e387a4d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3a1f96d-9a3f-5c1c-ab2d-e8d5123ffa2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459569} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b30ceda-f821-5ff6-ad47-743f125d45a9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460651} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4febe7f-c323-5336-a0c7-06d8d2c80d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459571} {:answer/id #uuid "9f13e4db-10a8-5be2-b31f-32fb5ffee6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f13e4db-10a8-5be2-b31f-32fb5ffee6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459572} :answer-field/id #uuid "28dc75a2-4163-519b-a815-660274285a03" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460652} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ad1a624-9364-5075-a11b-32a164535568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459573} {:answer/id #uuid "bf8a8f78-6603-53fe-bea5-f58fe4983bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf8a8f78-6603-53fe-bea5-f58fe4983bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459574} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7ab65c-c9c2-5efe-9a3a-405cb3117a7d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98524e7d-f97e-5125-b634-0488b239406e" :question/math-academy-id "q-156152" :question/problem "![](images/96/9685260f754e0d6333de6f24ffbf3908c055d6e4fe2d3ffe47391410735c7df2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[-2,1)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $A$ is not an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $A$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/39/39f7097f82f19d703208f9e4e8638e4d64aa417ad9cf4bc1f90092eb0c5e5639.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/3f/3ffa79d3052247aecd276c7794a498340acb66a1e74aeddcdd7800b1e9704506.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/f1/f116e950d99e0a00908c6c40a3c788cc450efa120843369e13f857cb084d8a1e.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f970e5b6-748b-5c4c-baed-ac5d23cd85b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449802} {:answer/id #uuid "30d06ec5-0d3e-55a1-b021-5f2fd6f47cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30d06ec5-0d3e-55a1-b021-5f2fd6f47cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449803} :answer-field/id #uuid "9225bd5d-1a3f-5113-9a58-8af2f115fadf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450855} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc2efc61-2ad8-57ee-92bc-c037458de584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449804} {:answer/id #uuid "f305cd93-59b0-5cc3-9cf2-f44e6f3f3c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f305cd93-59b0-5cc3-9cf2-f44e6f3f3c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449805} :answer-field/id #uuid "eca05e69-25e6-56a1-b759-23098e476289" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450856} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b095fc6-eef9-5f6b-8fce-f78d182c6c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449806} {:answer/id #uuid "2798cbce-ddaf-5a32-a92d-519f34eead10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2798cbce-ddaf-5a32-a92d-519f34eead10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449807} :answer-field/id #uuid "299aa1ed-32fb-596a-8c5d-345982ea9f21" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7714139-ef1f-551b-b703-2555d562d417" :question/math-academy-id "q-158536" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le 2},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(2,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(-2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=2.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/de/de056be6665a342dc8569e0db330743a96e08fa770798c94ec7dd0cc4e987d92.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(2,2)$ and $Q(-2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/c6/c6c1237dfa5f8fed76bad01466c94442d27825c4edab7dbc92a99b177af4a0b6.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the equation $y=2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le 2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. $Q$ is an interior point of $A$ since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained in $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca900fdb-2d62-5862-8f58-5e2c93efbb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453436} {:answer/id #uuid "839c8d82-74a4-5464-b707-309afd311002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "839c8d82-74a4-5464-b707-309afd311002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453437} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e5b6f56-ac9e-5312-8788-c82ee0344df5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454468} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb2be68-e58e-5cf5-a802-398a76d09aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453438} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1f7b94-44bb-5eab-8fad-f6e5da807f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae1f7b94-44bb-5eab-8fad-f6e5da807f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453439} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d1f656-b70c-5cbc-b720-c740ebcddde4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454469} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66605df2-8aaf-504d-949d-2dc2c5eb81cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453440} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb9751c-c1ad-5871-aa80-62619467576e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbb9751c-c1ad-5871-aa80-62619467576e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453441} :answer-field/id #uuid "54080e51-b892-57e7-b433-ee3c85f02bbe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbe53a96-4c4c-5897-ba48-d4861ec6cb49" :question/math-academy-id "q-158547" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y>2x-3},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2x-3}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $Q(0,0)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=2x-3.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/5a/5af40a400e5ea4942a1cf89b09184a277df96d252e4a7fee41bd2df7024a631f.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/27/27b978864eac65c93c7886b2d54dfede7130f0f93a16ef96421cd0e5741a6940.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=2x-3.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the coordinates of $Q(0,0)$ satisfy the inequality $y>2x-3.$ Hence, $Q$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a2b1785-6973-5d01-9c9d-ae0b2c5f9ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560526} {:answer/id #uuid "758ca936-3175-5b9a-a7f2-59300deea008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560616} {:answer/id #uuid "7933ca3c-2fce-5e9a-b5e4-ac56a04b4c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560624} {:answer/id #uuid "7c7d6717-571c-5bc0-bcde-f66a2294e7d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560630} {:answer/id #uuid "8b448c1a-f75e-567b-bba2-0c1b4f290c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c7d6717-571c-5bc0-bcde-f66a2294e7d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560630} :answer-field/id #uuid "7274d4e1-605f-55f2-a572-987750313a00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d9ea65f-7a2f-51be-a1e0-0db423dddcbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-158557" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le 2-x\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2-x\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(0,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(0,2)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(2,1)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=2-x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/ab/ab319d348d61ba7d247b21553de0625f8c4f9906800102a6f8e11264472cab13.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,2)$ and $Q(2,1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/9e/9ebf116e1093aa4861de907ed9256ec52e4a5b3e0765a6ab66cc8ee0b40e1769.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(0,2)$ satisfy the equation $y=2-x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0,2)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le 2-x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fb12b54-0323-5408-9f63-bd359e7b2e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453448} {:answer/id #uuid "164d2254-207a-594f-a70d-382d51b3f8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "164d2254-207a-594f-a70d-382d51b3f8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453449} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5032cdf-c59e-5e33-880d-955f6d02841e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454474} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3427dac4-97f1-5765-aafc-94068cf9677e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453450} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0ad7a7-e7bc-5678-aca2-5d4474e68307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3427dac4-97f1-5765-aafc-94068cf9677e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453450} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3387072-c1bc-59ee-8d45-dc6cd0e5411d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454475} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ad1ee2-a34e-5097-bfd1-c65783c31fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453452} {:answer/id #uuid "0e0e000c-633e-51cc-bcaf-35de257a2bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42ad1ee2-a34e-5097-bfd1-c65783c31fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453452} :answer-field/id #uuid "feaa10ce-c2f4-5d9a-998c-4aa5896d0d74" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e4c0564-b5b1-55bc-bc17-3dffa3a951a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158601" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<-1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=-1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(1,-1)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=-1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/75/75d5d199d12cc2f3170822a40c52762fcc8368caac26a8134661f9ac72ab462b.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/81/818da293be040897d3a536b396d6fe7ff992f3d91f976301e14a6253356b5bde.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=-1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<-1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db30fee-97ef-5c82-b9f7-e59f87e2d6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560431} {:answer/id #uuid "3c23e6dc-98f3-56bf-9dec-3f31245a5e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560501} {:answer/id #uuid "98623bac-9b60-5778-9acc-c9d6970dc0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560676} {:answer/id #uuid "bfee3041-1ea5-5d62-91c3-245076039872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560750} {:answer/id #uuid "e6644f14-7427-5268-94b7-90f4b0379b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfee3041-1ea5-5d62-91c3-245076039872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560750} :answer-field/id #uuid "374832d9-2085-56d7-b8ec-ffec23c86eb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb3ee0d5-b81d-5d00-99b5-5160f6c70200" :question/math-academy-id "q-158732" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y>\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(0,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(0,1)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(2,\\frac{4}{3})$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nThe set $A$ consists of all points that lie to the left of the parabola ${y}^{2}=4x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/8f/8f182873a8384097b56152284e67189e36f0a79a0b2bbda8926a2f8279db7ddd.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,1)$ and $Q(2,\\frac{4}{3})$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/66/66c8cb59cceadd43e3c4316925f1dd624c2e77a6ec547c9f9a1a02846bb537fb.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(0,1)$ do not satisfy the equation $y=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of the set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0,1)$ satisfy the inequality $y>\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ff2597b-4563-52dd-97bd-79931863e5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471089} {:answer/id #uuid "c64121cc-495f-5d02-8baa-6347a5757f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c64121cc-495f-5d02-8baa-6347a5757f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471090} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5738039-4384-58fe-ba54-1ac5ac80b370" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472050} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ba8d4db-3185-546a-9558-c0532b074b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471091} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c061e7-01b4-514b-badc-0fe24712237a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5c061e7-01b4-514b-badc-0fe24712237a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471092} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c6223b-9267-5508-b98c-7c2d2e8d01e6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472051} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "668d955d-390f-5905-adc9-a8877fa264d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471093} {:answer/id #uuid "e62939fe-e47a-5ae6-8bda-ff784b7b4a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "668d955d-390f-5905-adc9-a8877fa264d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471093} :answer-field/id #uuid "197eb59a-025c-5dfb-8852-a076b6a377f3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82163da2-d426-5778-8627-90a3d54226a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-159253" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3ea4dd786ab1ff777c7667bb94df36e2c04ceaefba8e4506754d1ec79d03641.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[-2,0)\\cup (0,2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/c9/c9acfd3e5e7d179904ba69d3473e5f8644055e777fa679e665cfb9199793d218.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ Notice that $B$ does not belong to $M$ and $B$ belongs to any open neighborhood of $B.$\n\n![](images/74/7480978db0850ff426ed4747ee7dfb81781ad7222f97fca0d6106c800d99fcbf.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $C$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/4f/4fb2567d76963e586370ee771f359b3ba051941d650d9d1f0dc58f47bb770ab4.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14858488-0968-53ee-a271-6b2c706d24f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560408} {:answer/id #uuid "1761a7c3-ecef-5d09-a98b-e38fb750a0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560416} {:answer/id #uuid "294aa78c-5727-5350-8592-0c1b6971026c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560453} {:answer/id #uuid "a07e75f8-1749-5ef7-a7ad-3f9a237b7f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560693} {:answer/id #uuid "c641877a-fed0-5681-9ab6-c88e09b14311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c641877a-fed0-5681-9ab6-c88e09b14311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560759} :answer-field/id #uuid "710f49fa-4048-565e-bf85-ef63e229f768" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37a7cf0c-c997-508e-b401-3de9aab0cfc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159255" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a50bd2b35b22d2ee71094467f1310f9c6c1510323e8106c13cf6fc45f2cbb9f.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[1,3]\\cup [4,6]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $A$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $A$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/0a/0a438012465859bc6406bb52a6bebeb13803760b429996fc7e4963b415c8f23d.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb374c99d9da28e40ad87dda6bf86dc6ca273a491e4faccb7dee043a104d0eba.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $C$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/a5/a58fac3f854cd89429090b7fd10140d26d2097372586cd5f130353bff338b97c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09cb1613-161a-586b-b39d-fa7423b2674b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560382} {:answer/id #uuid "c478305f-33cc-5b5e-b111-aa60762b3eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560756} {:answer/id #uuid "d1bf783b-f62d-5544-9b6f-a82c84002604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560782} {:answer/id #uuid "df46c36c-d040-5e4b-9960-bbaee7fd9b73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560803} {:answer/id #uuid "e3286edb-fba7-50de-891f-e39e3ed3ca07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1bf783b-f62d-5544-9b6f-a82c84002604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560782} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebcf0a61-7a0a-54a8-a956-1b1805e9c49e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b96b52d-bf1b-5917-8deb-641e04d4e427" :question/math-academy-id "q-159273" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d60800fb2b986aac396a278599651b9ee116339d4b5bbab9a7a6ae5e16f6a3b2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-2,0)\\cup \\{1\\}$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a boundary  point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/b5/b57ab5bfd2f0d685e2918c2f62601b9439475e7f29e2fbdabb912c43411a829f.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/f5/f5a2dc9c52c0c24b2f611cfd3b5aa23fb068249780fd66d80039b7d548c6151a.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* an interior point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/3b/3b47e92adb22c8d68cf94f947870d185ef907f9ca17c7427843e03fbcdab21cc.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/i