"b58541fc-27f2-5130-a4c2-74d24b8dffa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472753} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d9085bd-5d71-5a86-abfb-326a5e169143" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7105850-e717-5535-8a28-db3aaf9f3031" :question/math-academy-id "q-159495" :question/problem "![](images/19/19174ec33e43c900773031043d5de200e892f5e3ee51704d0800b6a944147155.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/43/43362800e91cbb57c22fc6851c10f00927a030a790dea9284e69a5c7c3d4ae25.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "573a6f2a-d19e-54b8-b52a-3fc5591ec0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477253} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0e6959-255c-5e11-8027-ae293adc5bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f0e6959-255c-5e11-8027-ae293adc5bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477254} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c9b4ae4-a89e-5884-92ef-182d3625cd79" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b61f44dc-82c7-5c0d-89cf-6055803222a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477255} {:answer/id #uuid "32559aea-2e15-5d47-af38-7adc87a8afe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32559aea-2e15-5d47-af38-7adc87a8afe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477256} :answer-field/id #uuid "be4e9bff-9352-5fd0-9f2c-87ba5edf7907" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477597} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "760ac135-7b58-53e7-97e0-76a624810edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477257} {:answer/id #uuid "bbeb8bd5-fb53-55a2-ad55-52063b47c939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "760ac135-7b58-53e7-97e0-76a624810edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477257} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bf00df5-4ad3-52cf-8c25-6fc675d3d709" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8871194-baee-5d59-8d60-7a847fcf31fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-36403" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le 2x-4},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2x-4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The point $P(2,0)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(2,0)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(1,3)$ is an interior point of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=2x-4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/b2/b2449c90a6565712b535df42840b8f874d6061ed240092b02ec5672152e125aa.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(2,0)$ and $Q(1,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/4a/4a176ab71239b0ec388d96f515b5e26767086ee37463119115dfe43ef190c506.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(2,0)$ satisfy the equation $y=2x-4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(2,0)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le 2x-4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23591f63-c86d-5f91-9a17-a690666923d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448578} {:answer/id #uuid "be31c6af-3a33-55f9-8fe1-63efb93650a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be31c6af-3a33-55f9-8fe1-63efb93650a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448579} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a890ed-051b-51e6-b54d-7c194bdaf7f9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e844f5b-4f06-5bf8-946e-b743a531b0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448580} {:answer/id #uuid "1847ce53-72b0-5d27-b50b-d4102ef44f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1847ce53-72b0-5d27-b50b-d4102ef44f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448581} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c644840-d5ba-52dd-ae49-d42747e8e8ca" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68a62ca0-3bd2-53c6-b360-1371ecc0ba04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448582} {:answer/id #uuid "587abaee-0245-5ac8-bcc3-421b0ac4056a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "587abaee-0245-5ac8-bcc3-421b0ac4056a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448583} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ebfedd-9fc3-5873-908e-1133f982c35c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b10043b-af03-5e4b-8ef7-b7ee2892ed6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36409" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/bb/bbc3e1bfb40c12cfdc655b4207c5f34ea0a01ce254b3f04b96b05ffbbad3f5af.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/10/100a2b2c1aa58cdfa59980cae4253e1de8b4b2c984c0793c66f7e7cc6edb5211.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13cc753c-b562-5cf3-bdc0-1f0bef4bdf5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560405} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe93c47-e51a-5c46-96e7-9cb51c84cb58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560434} {:answer/id #uuid "75523642-a8e9-5c20-af07-5cbd062f55b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560615} {:answer/id #uuid "d56409a1-dd3e-5129-b768-c2b620b4e283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560787} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e84243-54fd-5434-a031-9ceaa4099bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75523642-a8e9-5c20-af07-5cbd062f55b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560615} :answer-field/id #uuid "b40b396f-afbe-53ce-beb3-0c52eeeedd6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b9e706a-7ed7-5d91-bf85-9f21697ce716" :question/math-academy-id "q-64996" :question/problem "![](images/74/74bbb3351cf6720121c6ce0fbe8f99fa1c65560edbe5e547ecfd007835a50b89.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/17/173a69de844fac6141fe2fb3c695d070750b9df29b91364f66a0c1da28998428.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce141660-9bda-5acc-9a0b-31d41347e2bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471477} {:answer/id #uuid "287b6d72-9962-57e1-a9da-dfee2551628f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "287b6d72-9962-57e1-a9da-dfee2551628f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471478} :answer-field/id #uuid "be4caeae-3a30-5feb-a2cf-5a156c3f7974" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472244} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38a73300-641f-5407-b3f4-8f357fe85d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471479} {:answer/id #uuid "15d9b14e-9a66-5c4f-b212-9a4c1df2304c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38a73300-641f-5407-b3f4-8f357fe85d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471479} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdc78f53-2967-5895-a127-2e9edae5311a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472245} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e5c543-06f6-52de-8da5-ae1935d181e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471481} {:answer/id #uuid "07d213c4-b711-5570-9c3b-bec90c86c6d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07d213c4-b711-5570-9c3b-bec90c86c6d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471482} :answer-field/id #uuid "85cd195a-49d4-5f8f-83a9-a08d3f58048b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afb4c2fe-2048-5475-a89e-bbe7b7062429" :question/math-academy-id "q-71689" :question/problem "![](images/94/94911d7be45c5572a4ce47ce95c81a66bf6276144eeace8dd74cac15646c2082.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/50/5024d8cf4c7a21667eb91dadee2d3368f1f9f3504e2a06f27b020c2d18804586.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $C$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8edf4398-806c-5b7c-8cca-451c1c85e2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477395} {:answer/id #uuid "848c568e-ad22-5e10-bfab-6bc1193645b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "848c568e-ad22-5e10-bfab-6bc1193645b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477396} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdd4190d-d31a-5d8d-8d65-eb1058748103" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477667} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ed8f22c-13e1-5da8-9a5d-47f257bc93d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477397} {:answer/id #uuid "ba03f66a-7846-546f-9a3b-a45c8353d1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ed8f22c-13e1-5da8-9a5d-47f257bc93d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477397} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8285640-90a8-5d6a-8986-03e13675ddfe" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477668} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2db774f-57ec-5bf9-85fb-fc0b16a6f005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477399} {:answer/id #uuid "8a77e4e9-8637-58b0-9118-180e885d83ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a77e4e9-8637-58b0-9118-180e885d83ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477400} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e5c59fd-a6e3-5509-8c7c-036709638fa6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e699ccb-274c-55bb-b36e-2ab5da35b953" :question/math-academy-id "q-71882" :question/problem "![](images/33/33d777f400b53d2272636fc9c465cf83c22932d8172c5d8a35bcdd2bdaae09a1.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- **II.** $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- **III.** $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/46/46670ef62c2afa2e5aa5c0eb4d1684493f35ab4f9a82aa5375994bf1cb2cb154.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8275c709-4ca8-5287-afb6-8d3b36f9186c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123897} {:answer/id #uuid "a75f4cb2-f139-58b9-aa04-4ed98561c8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123898} {:answer/id #uuid "096c8f5d-b6c7-5d85-9db1-f7584ff8b60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123899} {:answer/id #uuid "33e178fa-54f8-5974-8e7a-f5f421266282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123900} {:answer/id #uuid "5766bcb7-fe05-5602-945f-985df6475722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5766bcb7-fe05-5602-945f-985df6475722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123901} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8eb2afe-d338-5aa0-9b42-4afd48839454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f3705f6-6b05-5981-9c91-c510c442e37e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71883" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(0.5,0.5)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0.5,0.5)$ and $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0.5,0.5)$  satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"}]}
